      "writtenOn": "2018-02-17T03:28:32Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Regarding tasks not tracking stats on CPUs that were never onlined when the task was created, is it for sure not a concern with Android? I thought there were some cases where the big CPUs were brought up sometime after userspace started up? Does it matter if the battery stats for some of the early threads isn\u0027t accurate? Does Google really look closely into those threads and try to optimize them? If it\u0027s not a metric that\u0027s tracked, it might not be a big issue to worry about.\n\nI\u0027m not against reallocating the array if we find more cpus have come online. It could help NUMA, etc. But I\u0027m sure there are going to be some architectures where this code isn\u0027t optimal. And if we make it optimal for those, it might be an overkill for Android. We could just say that this module isn\u0027t mandatory and they don\u0027t need to use it.\n\nFor example, this code never frees a \"all_freqs[cpu]\" if the CPU/policy goes offline. What if a CPU is physically removed in a server and never replaced. We\u0027ll be wasting memory for that CPU/cluster permanently. I\u0027m sure someone will complain about that.\n\nI\u0027m sure someone else will complain about cacheline thrashing in some case where NR_CPU is 1000 and we haven\u0027t declared the array as a \"per_cpu\" variable. But I\u0027m not sure we can avoid cache line thrashing since \"all_freqs[]\" for all CPUs in the same cluster point to the same memory location.\n\nLong story short, if we push this upstream we should try to claim that this will work for every architecture. We should upstream it as something meant for mobile devices that need finer tracking of CPU usage. If anyone wants to improve on this, they are welcome to.",
      "revId": "25866078274b014a87e4b03f112d6ee0329a4414",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
